{#
    A picture, and the claim under it.

    The core writes a bare `<figure>` with the image inside it and a
    `<figcaption>` under it. Correct markup, and nothing in this system. No
    frame around the picture, and no ground under one that does not fill it. A
    caption set as prose at the size of the prose beside it. That is the one
    thing this theme exists to stop. So the node comes out as `sds-figure`,
    which decides those three for everything else that shows a picture.

    The finish step draws the picture into the page — `soul-finish.js`, over
    the rendered output — and not a template that writes the element's own
    markup. That is what lets this address the component. A figure whose
    picture waits for a script is a caption under an empty box for a reader
    who runs none. By the time the page goes out there is no wait left.

    `sds-art` is what makes it sit in the frame. One rule shared by the drawing
    and the photograph, because how a picture fills its surface is the same
    question for both. `align` does not come through: a figure in a measure
    this narrow has nothing to float beside, which is the same answer the
    sidebar gets.

    Nothing here says which mode the picture arrives in, because there is only
    one. Every picture is a link, so a drawing renders in a document of its
    own and keeps the colours its file declares. It does not follow the reader
    into dark, and the frame under it does not either — see `--surface-art`.
    The published rule says why the alternative loses.

    A file somewhere else is a link whatever it is: a reference reads the file
    itself, which a browser will not do across origins.
#}
{%- set caption = node.document ? renderNode(node.document)|striptags|trim : '' -%}
{%- set source = node.image.value is external_target ? node.image.value : asset(node.image.value) -%}
{#- A picture draws at the width of the column it stands in, which is not
    the width a diagram exists for. `:zoomable:` gives that back. The frame
    becomes the press and the viewer carries the claim into its own head. The
    author writes it, because most pictures read where they stand. The option
    comes off the figure. The core hands the image node a fixed list, and one
    it has never heard of is not on it. -#}
<sds-figure
    src="{{ source }}"
    alt="{{ node.image.option('alt')|default('') }}"
    {%- if caption %} caption="{{ caption }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.image.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.image.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('zoomable') %} zoomable{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('figclass') %} class="{{ node.image.option('figclass') }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-figure>
